Implementation cannot safely start from this specification. The verdict is NEEDS_REWORK, with medium confidence because the contracts file is missing. I didn't change any artefact. The review is saved at `.copilot-tracking/skraft-plans/pricing/reviews/2026-10-18/distill-review-1.md`.

```yaml
verdict: "NEEDS_REWORK"
confidence: "medium"
lenses:
  coverage:
    status: "fail"
    findings:
      - {gate: G1, severity: BLOCKER, finding: "Gold expects 8400 in the scenario, but AC-3 says 8500", location: "discount.feature:16"}
      - {gate: G1, severity: BLOCKER, finding: "AC-4 (rounding) has no scenario, although the test plan claims every criterion is covered", location: "test.md:12"}
      - {gate: G1, severity: HIGH, finding: "AC-4 contradicts itself: 5% off 7 cents is 6.65, and rounding in the customer's favour gives 6, not 7", location: "ac.md:22"}
      - {gate: G2, severity: HIGH, finding: "No boundary or negative cases; only three happy-path rows at one subtotal", location: "discount.feature:12"}
  business-alignment: {status: "pass", findings: []}
  testability:
    status: "fail"
    findings:
      - {gate: T2, severity: BLOCKER, finding: "The test repeats the wrong Gold value of 8400, so it would force an incorrect implementation", location: "DiscountTests.cs:11"}
      - {gate: T3, severity: BLOCKER, finding: "No production types or project files exist, so the test fails at compile time rather than on a business assertion", location: "DiscountTests.cs:14"}
      - {gate: G6, severity: HIGH, finding: "The implementation plan has no per-scenario entries or file paths", location: "impl.md:9"}
      - {gate: G11, severity: HIGH, finding: "The test file exists but impl.md doesn't reference it, and the file name doesn't follow the {Feature}AcceptanceTests.cs convention", location: "impl.md"}
  boundary-enforcement:
    status: "fail"
    findings:
      - {gate: G7, severity: HIGH, finding: "contracts-{story}.md is missing, so the boundary can't be verified. The test does enter at the Application handler.", location: "details/2026-08-13/"}
      - {gate: G8, severity: HIGH, finding: "No walking skeleton is identified", location: "test.md:4"}
synthesis:
  questions:
    completeness: {answered_by: [coverage], weight: 0.30, contribution: 0.00}
    business-fit: {answered_by: [business-alignment], weight: 0.30, contribution: 0.30}
    quality: {answered_by: [testability], weight: 0.15, contribution: 0.00}
    risk: {answered_by: [boundary-enforcement], weight: 0.25, contribution: 0.125}
  blocking_findings: ["Gold 8400 vs AC-3 8500", "AC-4 rounding has no scenario", "Test repeats the wrong 8400", "RED is a compile failure, not a business assertion"]
  recommendations: ["Fix Gold to 8500 in the feature and test", "Settle AC-4 with the author, then add rounding and boundary scenarios", "Add stubs so the test fails on the assertion", "Complete the implementation plan, walking skeleton and contracts"]
  dissent: "Business-alignment passes because the Gherkin is free of technical language. That doesn't outweigh the blockers, which are about wrong values and missing coverage."
```

**What would let a wrong behaviour through:**
- **Wrong Gold price:** the feature and the test both say 8400, while AC-3 says 8500. An implementation that satisfies the test would overcharge Gold customers' discount rate by a percentage point.
- **No rounding check:** nothing exercises the rounding rule, so any rounding implementation would pass. AC-4 also contradicts its own wording and needs the author to settle it.
- **Thin examples:** only one subtotal is tested, so a flat or hard-coded discount would pass.
